WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.230
Twenty quiz questions on healthy

2
00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:03.910
eating, in ten seconds!

3
00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.020
Then I read them.

4
00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:09.430
One says rice is a body-building food.

5
00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.920
The examples are pizza and blueberries.

6
00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:15.740
And the words are far too

7
00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:17.020
hard for Primary Four.

8
00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:21.130
Today: generate, then check, before

9
00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:22.890
anything reaches your pupils.

10
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:27.200
AI is a fast first-draft machine:

11
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.280
questions, examples, explanations, in

12
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:31.700
seconds.

13
00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:34.690
But it predicts what sounds likely,

14
00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:36.330
and it can’t see your class.

15
00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:39.150
So everything it makes is a draft

16
00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:40.530
until you’ve checked it.

17
00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:42.890
Check four things.

18
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.240
Facts: is it true?

19
00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:46.760
Check the textbook.

20
00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:49.500
Level: are the words and

21
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.320
difficulty right for this age?

22
00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:54.550
Bias: who’s in it, who’s left

23
00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:56.770
out, is every doctor a man?

24
00:00:57.470 --> 00:00:59.470
Local fit: our foods,

25
00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:01.950
names, money and seasons.

26
00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.420
You can also ground it in your own source.

27
00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.380
Paste in a textbook page and say:

28
00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.400
“Answer only from this page.

29
00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:12.740
If it isn’t there, say so.”

30
00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.240
Now answers should come from a

31
00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.080
page you trust and can check.

32
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:20.830
That’s called grounding: tying an

33
00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:22.570
answer to a source you give it.

34
00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:24.820
Some tools search your documents

35
00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:27.140
first, then answer from what they

36
00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:28.600
found and show where.

37
00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:30.530
That’s called RAG:

38
00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:32.410
retrieval-augmented generation.

39
00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:35.090
Think of the school newsletter.

40
00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:37.660
A volunteer can draft it fast.

41
00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.370
But before it goes home to parents,

42
00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:43.330
the head teacher reads every line,

43
00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:45.090
because her name is on it.

44
00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:47.850
AI drafts; you’re the editor.

45
00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:49.710
Quick check!

46
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:53.560
I told the AI: “Answer only

47
00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:55.060
from this textbook page.”

48
00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.600
Is its answer now guaranteed correct?

49
00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:02.210
No: still check it against the page.

50
00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:04.360
Grounding makes mistakes

51
00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:06.200
rarer and easier to spot.

52
00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:09.330
But it can still misread the page,

53
00:02:09.730 --> 00:02:12.150
mix in other things it learned, or

54
00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:13.110
quote the wrong part.

55
00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:15.690
And if the page is wrong or out

56
00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:17.430
of date, so is the answer.

57
00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.300
A story where every doctor is a man

58
00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.100
and every cook a woman: fix it.

59
00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:27.540
Prices in dollars and apples, not

60
00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.000
naira and mangoes: change them.

61
00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:32.710
A Primary Two text with words like

62
00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:35.150
“precipitation”: simplify it.

63
00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:37.240
Each fix takes a minute.

64
00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:40.550
In the app, ask about a school:

65
00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:43.530
exams, the bus, lunch.

66
00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:46.430
The AI searches the school’s documents,

67
00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:49.470
then quotes the one it used, or says it

68
00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:50.870
can’t find it instead of guessing.

69
00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:53.480
Compare the answer without sources,

70
00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:56.240
then edit a document and ask again.

71
00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:58.000
Your turn.

72
00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:01.170
An AI drafted my worksheet, and a

73
00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:02.450
mistake reached my pupils.

74
00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:05.040
Whose responsibility is it?

75
00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:09.420
Amina’s: a person stays responsible for

76
00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.260
everything that reaches pupils.

77
00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:14.840
Remember: AI drafts; you check

78
00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:18.460
facts, level, bias and local fit.

79
00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:21.290
Grounding helps, but you still check.

80
00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:24.430
Generate fast, then check carefully.

81
00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:27.070
Try it in class tomorrow:

82
00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:29.310
play “Find the mistakes”.

83
00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:32.060
Give pairs an AI-made worksheet

84
00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:33.940
with a few planted mistakes.

85
00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:35.970
They check it against the

86
00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:38.230
textbook and circle every error.

87
00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:40.410
The best evidence wins,

88
00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:41.830
not the fastest pair.
